# =============================================================================
# OT/ICS Security Lab — Phase 2 step 3a local rules
#
# Recon-detection ruleset (5 rules) targeting the traffic Phase 2 scenario 1
# generated: nmap host discovery + TCP connect-scan + service-version probes.
# SID range 9000000–9000999 reserved for this lab's local rules.
#
# Mode: IDS (alert only). No drops, no flow modification.
#
# Cross-references:
#   docs/phase2/scenarios/scenario-1-recon-nmap.md   (attack)
#   docs/monitoring.md §5                            (indicator table)
#   docs/MASTER.md Phase 2 Findings §3               (recon finding)
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000001 — ICMP echo-request burst from a single source.
# Trigger: 5 or more ICMP echo-requests from one src in a 10-second window.
# Why: nmap -sn (host discovery) sends ICMP echo-request to every address
#      in the target range. The lab's normal baseline has zero ICMP traffic
#      so any burst is anomalous. 5/10s threshold tolerates ad-hoc ping
#      checks while flagging sweeps.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert icmp any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"OTLAB-RECON ICMP echo-request sweep (host discovery)"; itype:8; threshold: type both, track by_src, count 5, seconds 10; classtype:network-scan; sid:9000001; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000002 — TCP SYN burst from a single source.
# Trigger: 10 or more TCP SYN packets from one src in 10 seconds.
# Why: nmap -sT (connect scan) opens a full TCP handshake to each probed
#      port. Even on a small target set (2 hosts x 8 ports = 16 probes),
#      that crosses 10/10s easily. Legitimate baseline (one long-lived
#      FUXA→openplc connection) generates zero new SYNs after startup.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"OTLAB-RECON TCP SYN scan (rate threshold)"; flags:S; threshold: type both, track by_src, count 10, seconds 10; classtype:network-scan; sid:9000002; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000003 — Modbus :502 contact from a non-FUXA source.
# Trigger: any TCP SYN to OpenPLC's Modbus port from a src NOT equal to
#      the pinned FUXA address.
# Why: docs/monitoring.md §5 row 2 — "Only FUXA should talk to the PLC."
#      An attacker on ot_zone trying to reach Modbus is detected on the
#      first SYN. Also catches host-side pymodbus tooling (per §5 row 2
#      definition; intentional).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp !$FUXA_IP any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-RECON TCP SYN to Modbus :502 from non-FUXA source"; flow:not_established,to_server; flags:S; classtype:network-scan; sid:9000003; rev:2;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000004 — OpenPLC web UI contact from a source that is NOT host-NAT'd
#           operator traffic.
# Trigger: TCP SYN to OpenPLC web ports (8080 or 8443) from any source IP
#      that is not one of the bridge-gateway addresses (172.18.0.1 /
#      172.19.0.1). Host-side browser access from the Windows operator
#      arrives at the container post-NAT with the bridge gateway as the
#      source IP — that's the only legitimate path to the web UI in this
#      lab. Anything else hitting 8080/8443 is a container-on-bridge
#      talking to OpenPLC's web UI, which no container should be doing
#      in normal operation. The lab's containers are FUXA (Modbus only,
#      doesn't speak HTTP to openplc), openplc itself, the attacker
#      (when boundary-crossed), and Suricata (passive).
# Why: Direct tie to Phase 2 Finding §3 (the documentation-drift
#      hardening trap). Rule covers BOTH 8080 and 8443 — Phase 1 docs
#      only listed 8080, so a Phase-3 firewall on 8080 would miss 8443;
#      this rule catches recon hitting either port.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp !$MGMT_OPERATOR any -> $OPENPLC_IP $OPENPLC_WEB_PORTS (msg:"OTLAB-RECON TCP SYN to OpenPLC web UI from non-operator source"; flow:not_established,to_server; flags:S; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:9000004; rev:2;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000005 — TCP RST burst from a single source (connection-churn signature).
# Trigger: 15 or more RST packets from one src in 10 seconds.
# Why: nmap connect-scan (-sT) closes each probed socket immediately,
#      producing RSTs both from closed-port responses and from the scanner
#      tearing down established sockets. A burst of RSTs from one src is
#      the classic port-scan churn signature (docs/monitoring.md §5 row 5).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"OTLAB-RECON TCP RST connection-churn (scan signature)"; flags:R; threshold: type both, track by_src, count 15, seconds 10; classtype:network-scan; sid:9000005; rev:1;)

# =============================================================================
# Phase 2 step 3b — Modbus protocol-aware detection (SIDs 9000010–9000015)
#
# Added 2026-05-24. Rules use Suricata's modbus app-layer keyword (verified
# against upstream 8.0.5 docs). All rules are protocol-aware and
# deterministic — no rate thresholds (per Finding §8).
#
# Coverage:
#   - 9000010, 9000011: baseline-unused FCs (FC 2, FC 4) — recon signal
#   - 9000012, 9000013: write to coils/HRs from non-FUXA source (any
#                       address — no legitimate writer exists)
#   - 9000014, 9000015: read outside documented map (address >= 3)
#
# Map cutoffs verified against plc/tank_fill.st:
#   - Coils 0,1,2 = Pump_Run, Valve_Open, High_Alarm
#   - HR 0,1,2 = Level_PV, SP_Low_Out, SP_High_Out
#   - Out-of-map = address >2 (per Finding §4: silent zeros, not exception 02)
#
# Cross-references:
#   docs/phase2/scenarios/scenario-{2,3,4,4b}-*.md  (attacks)
#   docs/monitoring.md §5 rows 1, 3, 6              (indicators)
#   docs/MASTER.md Phase 2 Findings §4, §6, §8      (design constraints)
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000010 — Modbus Read Discrete Inputs (FC 2) from any source.
# Trigger: any FC 2 PDU to OpenPLC:502.
# Why: monitoring.md §4 baseline = FC 1 + FC 3 only. FC 2 has zero
#      legitimate use in this lab — any FC 2 = recon / FC enumeration.
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (function 2). Deterministic.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp any any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Read Discrete Inputs (FC 2) — not used by baseline"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: function 2; classtype:protocol-command-decode; sid:9000010; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000011 — Modbus Read Input Registers (FC 4) from any source.
# Trigger: any FC 4 PDU to OpenPLC:502.
# Why: same baseline-violation logic as 9000010. .st declares no input
#      registers; FUXA never reads FC 4.
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (function 4). Deterministic.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp any any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Read Input Registers (FC 4) — not used by baseline"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: function 4; classtype:protocol-command-decode; sid:9000011; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000012 — Modbus write to coil (FC 5/15) from non-FUXA source.
# Trigger: any FC 5 (write single coil) or FC 15 (write multiple coils)
#      PDU from a source IP that is NOT FUXA.
# Why: monitoring.md §5 row 1 — no legitimate writer exists in this lab.
#      Address is not the signal; the combination of source-IP + write-class
#      IS. Mapped coils 0,1,2 (Pump/Valve/Alarm) are the operational-
#      deception target (Finding §6: alarm-coil falsification produces
#      phantom HMI alarm). Writes to unmapped coils are protocol-surface
#      enumeration recon — also worth flagging. The msg groups FC 5 + FC 15
#      since the security signal is identical; the pcap distinguishes them
#      for forensics.
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (access write coils, no address filter).
#      Suricata 8.0.5 modbus keyword's address <N comparator on access
#      write rules is silently off-by-one (matches <(N-1)). Address
#      filtering on writes dropped in favor of source-IP + write-class
#      as the robust primitive. Full investigation in
#      docs/phase2/detection/3b-modbus-protocol.md.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp !$FUXA_IP any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Write to coil from non-FUXA source"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: access write coils; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:9000012; rev:3;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000013 — Modbus write to holding register (FC 6/16) from non-FUXA source.
# Trigger: any FC 6 (write single register) or FC 16 (write multiple
#      registers) PDU from a source IP that is NOT FUXA.
# Why: monitoring.md §5 row 1 + Phase 1 §5.3 (HR writes accepted, PLC
#      reasserts every scan). HR 0,1,2 = Level / SP_Low_Out / SP_High_Out;
#      falsifying corrupts HMI display but PLC control unaffected (internal
#      SP_Low / SP_High live outside Modbus map). Writes to unmapped
#      registers are protocol-surface enumeration — also flagged. Address
#      is not the signal; source-IP + write-class IS.
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (access write holding, no address filter).
#      See 9000012 for the off-by-one investigation that motivated
#      dropping the address sub-option.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp !$FUXA_IP any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Write to holding register from non-FUXA source"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: access write holding; classtype:attempted-admin; sid:9000013; rev:3;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000014 — Modbus read of coil where read range overlaps OUT-OF-MAP space.
# Trigger: FC 1 (Read Coils) where any address in the read range falls in
#      3..65535 (i.e. the read touches at least one unmapped coil). Any src.
# Why: monitoring.md §5 row 6 (reads outside the map). Per Finding §4,
#      OpenPLC returns silent zeros (not exception 02) for out-of-map
#      reads — exception-watching is dead; address inspection only. NOT
#      source-IP filtered: FUXA drifting outside the map is also a useful
#      (config-drift) signal. Boundary-crossing reads (e.g. read addr=0
#      qty=6 spans coils 0..5, includes unmapped 3,4,5) are correctly
#      flagged — an attacker enumerating beyond the map.
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (access read coils, address 3<>65535).
#      Suricata 8.0.5 modbus keyword: address X<>Y matches if ANY address
#      in the read range falls in [X,Y]. Comparator forms (>N, <N) were
#      empirically found to no-op-match-all on read rules; range form is
#      the working option.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp any any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Read coil range overlaps out-of-map (>=3)"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: access read coils, address 3<>65535; classtype:protocol-command-decode; sid:9000014; rev:2;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000015 — Modbus read of holding register where read range overlaps OUT-OF-MAP.
# Trigger: FC 3 (Read Holding Registers) where any address in the read
#      range falls in 3..65535. Any source.
# Why: same as 9000014. Scenario 2 exercises this with FC 3 reads at HR
#      100 and HR 1000 (fully out-of-map). The FC 3 read at HR 0 qty 3
#      (covers 0..2, fully in-map) is correctly EXCLUDED — confirms the
#      range form's "any-addr-in-range" semantic doesn't over-match
#      legitimate mapped-region reads.
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (access read holding, address 3<>65535). See
#      9000014 for the address-comparator-vs-range empirical finding.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp any any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Read holding register range overlaps out-of-map (>=3)"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: access read holding, address 3<>65535; classtype:protocol-command-decode; sid:9000015; rev:2;)

# =============================================================================
# Phase 2 step 3c — Non-FUXA Modbus reads (SIDs 9000020–9000021)
#
# Added 2026-05-25. Symmetric companions to 9000012 / 9000013 (non-FUXA
# writes). Alert on FC 1 (read coils) and FC 3 (read holding) from any
# source that is NOT FUXA — the Modbus-layer companion to 9000003's L4
# alert on non-FUXA contact with :502.
#
# These are NOT replay-detection rules. They detect unauthorized Modbus
# reads from a non-FUXA source. A replay attack (verbatim or mutate-and-
# replay of a captured FUXA PDU) is ONE INSTANCE of an unauthorized read,
# so these rules will fire on the read portion of a replay — but they
# will equally fire on any other unauthorized read (recon, data-exfil,
# operator monitoring from the wrong host). The detection capability is
# "non-FUXA read," not "replay."
#
# Coverage analysis vs. existing rules:
#   - Every flow that fires 9000020 / 9000021 also fires 9000003 at the
#     SYN. These rules add Modbus-layer evidence (app_proto: modbus +
#     read operation) to the L4 SYN alert; they do NOT add new detection
#     coverage. Framing is forensic granularity, not new detection.
#   - Symmetric with 9000012 / 9000013: completes the source-IP-filtered
#     by-table set (read coils, read holding, write coils, write holding).
#   - 9000014 / 9000015 already alert on out-of-map reads from ANY source.
#     9000020 / 9000021 fire on IN-MAP reads from non-FUXA — a gap those
#     don't cover.
#
# Keyword choice: no address sub-clause, same as 9000012 / 9000013. The
# detection signal is source-IP-not-FUXA + read-class. Address is
# irrelevant — an attacker reading mapped HR 0..2 is exactly as
# unauthorized as reading unmapped HR 1000. (Suricata 8.0.5 address-
# comparator bugs on read rules per 3b Keyword Finding §3 would also
# constrain address use here; not relevant given the signal choice.)
#
# Scenario-5 replay attack — these rules add Modbus-layer evidence to
# the existing 9000003 + 9000013 coverage. Per Replay Finding §1 in
# docs/phase2/detection/3c-replay-attack.md: source IP is the only
# protocol-layer signal that distinguishes FUXA from a clever attacker;
# the actual control for replay is segmentation, not signature
# detection.
#
# Cross-references:
#   docs/phase2/scenarios/scenario-5-modbus-replay.md  (attack)
#   docs/phase2/detection/3c-replay-attack.md          (3c evidence)
#   docs/monitoring.md §5 row 2                        (indicator)
#   docs/MASTER.md Phase 2 Findings §11, §12           (3c detection + platform-constraint findings)
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000020 — Modbus read of coil (FC 1) from non-FUXA source.
# Trigger: any FC 1 (read coils) PDU from a source IP that is NOT FUXA.
# Why: monitoring.md §5 row 2 — FUXA is the only legitimate Modbus
#      client. Any non-FUXA source reading coils is unauthorized
#      enumeration of process state (Pump_Run / Valve_Open / High_Alarm
#      plus protocol-surface beyond the documented map). Adds protocol-
#      layer evidence (app_proto: modbus + read coils operation) to the
#      L4 alert from 9000003. NOT replay-specific (see section header).
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (access read coils). No address sub-clause —
#      see section header for rationale.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp !$FUXA_IP any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Read coil from non-FUXA source"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: access read coils; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:9000020; rev:2;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9000021 — Modbus read of holding register (FC 3) from non-FUXA source.
# Trigger: any FC 3 (read holding registers) PDU from a source IP that
#      is NOT FUXA.
# Why: monitoring.md §5 row 2 — same logic as 9000020. HR 0,1,2 expose
#      tank Level_PV plus operator-display setpoints; non-FUXA reads
#      here reveal current process state to an unauthorized client.
#      Verbatim replay of a FUXA FC 3 poll from an attacker source IP is
#      ONE INSTANCE of this — the rule is not specific to replay. NOT
#      replay-specific (see section header).
# Keyword: modbus app-layer (access read holding). No address sub-clause —
#      see section header for rationale.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tcp !$FUXA_IP any -> $OPENPLC_IP $MODBUS_PORT (msg:"OTLAB-MODBUS Read holding register from non-FUXA source"; flow:to_server,established; modbus: access read holding; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:9000021; rev:2;)
